Z||000004||Bill H||10-19-2000||10:03 PM||||do you know where to get one and what it costs?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||10-19-2000||10:15 PM||||Last I heard, LSD's for the Getrag are around 2-3 grand, if you can find them.  <P>For the Isuzu go to <A HREF="http://www.isuzuperformance.com/bills/drivdiff.html#quaife" TARGET=_blank>Bill's Isuzu Performance</A>.  About $1,300 for a Quaife limited slip.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||10-19-2000||11:47 PM||||Last time I checked GM, the Getrag LSD was down to about $1700.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||10-19-2000||11:56 PM||||I have an 85SE v-6 4spd.  Although it is not a limited slip differential when I get on it strong I lay two patches of rubber just like a LSD.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||10-20-2000||01:11 AM||||Two strips of rubber is because the Fiero uses a regular FWD tranny. There isn't any side loading a normal RWD vehicle experiences, so for most applications, LS isn't necessary.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pffsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||the need for speed||10-20-2000||02:54 AM||||sorry but what is limited slip differentail????????? and whats it good for?||||reg||1
Z||000010||862M4inCA||10-20-2000||05:02 AM||||Actually guys, I recall a post a few months back about this exact topic...Don't recall who made the point, however it was said that due to the transversly mounted powertrain the fiero uses, it doesn't allow any left/right torque to be applied to the drive wheels therefor both wheels will spin at exactly the same speed (stomp on the pedal and lay two lines of rubber). The posi system was mostly used on cars that used a conventional setup where torque from the motor put more weight on one side and eased up the weight on the other side making the "lighter" side slip easier and laying one one line of rubber.  Since we Fiero owners have our engines transversly mounted, the only movement from the motor in front to back, and not side to side. When you rev the motor up, it torques to the rear of the car, putting and equal amount of weight on each rear wheel, thereby eleminating the need for limited slip equipment.<P>&lt;the need for speed&gt; in a posi-track differential, aside from having the typical ring and pinion gears, they also include a series of clutches to "detect" when one wheel is spinning excessively faster than the other, and lock up, allowing both wheels to spin at equal speeds. This is not necessary on transversly mounted powertrains. Basically, limited slip differentials are desired when you want to apply equal amounts of power to both driving wheels. Having one wheel spin while the other sits stationary isn't a good thing, especially when control is an issue. (ie. one wheel on pavement, the other on soft sand) <P>Hope this answers your questions guys.<p>[This message has been edited by 862M4inCA (edited 10-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Rodney||10-20-2000||08:29 AM||||Only reason to install a limited slip in a Fiero is if you road course race and need to put a lot of power down in the turns. I have the stage II turbo on mine and have not seen a need for a limited slip. Mantapart makes a nice home built unit with clutch packs for about $750.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||rockcrawl||10-20-2000||10:34 PM||||I agree the only reason a LSD (not the drug!) would be required in a Fiero is for autocrossing. I've never experienced traction problems with a Fiero on the street. Contact one of the guys at Team Python for info on the Gleason Torsen diff, that's what they are using. <A HREF="http://www.azstarnet.com/~gduncan/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.azstarnet.com/~gduncan/index.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||10-21-2000||06:12 PM||||Even in a autocross car you only need limited slip if you have a tendancy to spin the inside wheel. Cars with high CG or a hell of allot of torque can do that. (Body roll will lighten the inside wheel)The limited slip will keep power going to the wheel with a bite. It's not doing anything unless one wheel breaks loose.<P>It would also help driving in crappy weather. (Which is what is was originally designed for) Helps keep you from getting stuck if one wheel is on ice or mud.||||reg||1
